        {
            "number": 2,
            "revision": "d36684b42011688225707d67cf0dff6fa801f394",
            "parents": [
                "fadfd2bdafe14d5584024755910fb8f94edaef71"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/12/88312/2",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Eric Yang",
                "email": "eric.yang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                "username": "fornchu110"
            },
            "createdOn": 1773749546,
            "author": {
                "name": "Eric Yang",
                "email": "eric.yang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                "username": "fornchu110"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "At a high level I think we need to update target-fail-monitoring.md out in the docs repo and I'd like to see an example of the json file change. Also, I agree in some cases an immediate quiesce makes sense but with some services, we actually don't want that. i.e a lot of services are built to recover fine after a systemd service restart. A BMC quiesce is a big deal and should be avoided in these cases."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Eric Yang",
                        "email": "eric.yang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "fornchu110"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for the detailed feedback.\nI've updated target-fail-monitoring.md in [88393] with the JSON format, design details, and testing steps. The full JSON example is also in the commit message here and [87257].\n\nFully agree that BMC quiesce should be avoided for services that recover fine \u2014 the two keys serve different severity levels: \"services\" only quiesces after all restart retries are exhausted, while \"immediate_quiesce_services\" fires on the first failure for services where even a brief crash causes irreparable damage. \n\nThe feature is configured per platform based on its own needs \u2014 [87257] set it up for yosemite4 as an example, though whether it stays long-term is still up to the platform maintainers.\n\n[88393]: https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/docs/+/88393\n[87257]: https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/openbmc/+/87257\n[88357]: https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/openbmc/+/88357"
                }
            ],
